--- a/asm/daemon/serve_child.c
+++ b/asm/daemon/serve_child.c
@@ child main (~line 101): store_init retry block + the IPC child spawn path.
  CORRECTED 2026-09-05 after grep evidence: across ALL console logs of the
  benchmark (4 boots), "store_init failed" appeared exactly ONCE, on ONE peer
  connect (13.221.17.158) at 05:00:1x during a SIGTERM window -- the parent
  was tearing down (mempool teardown, fee-estimator flush, child SIGTERMs)
  when that connect landed. My earlier claim of a deterministic per-peer
  failure ("8/53 children die at boot") was WRONG: the child does not open
  the UTXO store at all (gettxout answers via IPC to the worker), and the
  "0 replies" stranger-probe failures have no store_init component.
  What is real:
    - one race: a connect accepted during shutdown runs store_init against
      a parent mid-teardown and dies. Cosmetic (server survived, peer got
      nothing), but a shutdown-window accept should be refused, not forked.
    - the FATAL-without-errno pattern stands as a diagnosability gap.
  Fix kept SMALL and honest: refuse new accepts once g_shutdown_requested
  is set (main.c's child reaper already sets the flag on stop), and log
  errno on failure. @@
     if(store_init(store_buf)!=0){
         usleep(50000);
         if(store_init(store_buf)!=0){
             usleep(100000);
             if(store_init(store_buf)!=0){
-                fprintf(stderr,"FATAL: store_init failed after 3 attempts\n");
+                fprintf(stderr,"FATAL: store_init failed after 3 attempts "
+                               "(errno=%d %s)\n", errno, strerror(errno));
                 _exit(1);
             }
         }
     }
@@ serve_mux accept site (main.c, the accept loop that forks children) @@
+        /* SC-1 (corrected): don't fork a child into a dying parent. The 09-05
+         * benchmark observed exactly one child die on store_init -- a peer
+         * connect accepted during the SIGTERM teardown window. Refusing
+         * (close immediately) during shutdown matches Core (StopNode skips
+         * new connections) and removes the whole class. */
+        if (g_shutdown_requested) { close(afd); continue; }
